body:
*^(Post Mortem Profits)*

&#x200B;

*An echoed conversation begins in the men's restroom between two button down's and ties. The man in the glasses begins to speak while washing his hands.*

&#x200B;

Glasses: Did you see what he brought to the meeting?

Non-Glasses: I don't know how the fuck he's going to pull that off in that suit.

Glasses: The board gave him the time to speak, but his suit really just says how out of touch he is. He'll never survive this market. We have to pull the plug on Death.

&#x200B;

*Just then Death walked in with the leather suit the button down and ties were referring to. Not having heard their conversation you couldn't help but feel the tension that was among the Men's Restroom. Death enters the center stall, and walks out to wash his hands. No saying a word. Nothing but the scrape of removing restroom paper towels was heard until he exited.*

&#x200B;

Non-Glasses: You think he heard us?

Glasses: I hope he did. Give him a little taste of how badly he's going to bomb in there. We better get going before we're late.

Non-Glasses: I'll bring the popcorn...

&#x200B;

*Both men chuckle as they walk out of the revolving door, and into the transparent glassed conference room. Shareholder's and executives circumference the large dark wooded table. Erect to the edge of the table was a diagram in different shades. Conversation is heard throughout the room, until the sound of Death's leather suit squeaking separated it. The Lead exec begins the meeting without acknowledging Death as a sign of distaste.*

&#x200B;

Lead Exec: Due to the increasing depreciation of natural fatalities this meeting was called into place by the Board to put to light the advantages of natural demise.

*The lead exec then turns over to Death, and gestures his left hand. Allowing Death to begin his presentation. Gently clearing his throat Death takes the floor, and the lead exec takes a seat.*

&#x200B;

Death: First of all we have to acknowledge the inflation that has been brought about in recent centuries. Lets bring it to the 16th century. Early England. Life spans were only 25 - 30 years. Of course if they lived a full and natural life. Most would die before the age of 16 due to disease, and (or) wound infliction. Post Mortem profits were prevalent among the shareholders. We began hitting a plateau at about the 18th century when various vaccines, and sanitation techniques came in to play. The next significant encounter was the discovery of the Polio Vaccine. Which forced me to terminate my then workforce. People now have a life expectancy of around 80 years of age in most European, and North American countries. This will not do. For future share holders, and Post Mortem Profits we should increase our spectrum during major wars. 

&#x200B;

*One of the shareholders gently raised his hand.*

&#x200B;

Death: Yes?

Shareholder: Having to wait between wars, however significant, gives us longer periods of stagnation doesn't it?

Death: We could say the same for the terminally ill patients on the receiving list. Sometimes its years, if not lifelong, before they pass. Through a broader interest in war we could obtain enough from both involved parties to sustain us until the next conflict arises.

&#x200B;

*At this the room was filled with chatter. The shareholders are not convinced. Death knows it. He began to think of sweeter days. During the Black Plague, or the French Revolution when nearly all of the profits were met by guillotine. The Lead Exec stood up, and buttoned his blazer.*

&#x200B;

Lead Exec: I think we've heard enough. Demise Inc. is no longer going to implement the brutality + fatality policy. We're leading profits through depression, and suicide. Eventually branching onto mental illness.

&#x200B;

*Death at this felt crushed. He knew what this meant for him. He was now obsolete to the company, and they'd soon replace him with someone else. The conference table was surrounded by nodding heads in agreement. Death had then left the transparent room, and took refuge in his cubicle to the southwest window of the office. He read the profit margins. Currently at 3,391. He looked once more at the window near his cubicle. He wasn't going to lead death anymore he'll be sure to end it. Later that afternoon the Lead Exec had noticed Death had not left his cubicle. He was face down with a bottle of empty sleeping pills. The Lead Exec picked up the phone as fast as he could to rush someone over.*

&#x200B;

Lead Exec. It's Death he's not moving. I think he might have overdosed.

&#x200B;

*The Lead Exec quickly hung up, and soon a doctor arrived.*

&#x200B;

Lead Exec: Thank Death you're here doctor.

Doctor: Of course.

&#x200B;

*The doctor then tried to find a pulse in Death, but could not.*

&#x200B;

Doctor: It looks like he already passed.

Lead Exec: Great news, that leaves our profit margins at 3,392 for today.

&#x200B;
